<Solution>

  <!--
    persistence-service.slnx

    The per service solution for the Persistence service of the PowerFramework .NET 10
    decomposition. Persistence is the ONLY service in the system that generates or executes SQL
    and the ONLY one that holds a storage provider. It listens on port 5101, speaks gRPC for
    contracts C-05 Query, C-06 Update, C-07 Command and C-08 Transaction, and exposes REST
    /health anonymously alongside /v1/ping behind a JWT. It validates tokens only and never mints
    one, because Security is the sole issuer in the system.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT BOILERPLATE
    This file is the mechanism that makes each service restore, build and test independently from
    a clean checkout. With exactly ONE solution file present in this directory, the documented per
    service command sequence resolves it automatically, with no extra argument:

        cd services/persistence-service
        dotnet restore
        dotnet build -c Release
        dotnet test, requesting the "XPlat Code Coverage" data collector

    That last line is named rather than spelled because the collector option begins with a double
    hyphen, which is illegal inside an XML comment and would make this file malformed.

    Solution discovery inspects the CURRENT directory only and never walks up. The repository root
    PowerFramework.slnx, which enumerates every project in the repository as a whole repo
    developer convenience, therefore neither supersedes nor interferes with this file. Conversely,
    adding a second solution file of any name beside this one would destroy that automatic
    resolution, so this directory holds exactly one.

    EXACTLY TWO PROJECTS, AND WHY THAT SET IS COMPLETE
    Only this service's own application and test projects are enumerated. The four shared
    libraries the application consumes, PowerFramework.Contracts, PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel,
    PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics and PowerFramework.Shared.Containers, are reached through
    the application project's own ProjectReference items. Restore and build follow project
    references regardless of solution membership, so those four are restored and built here
    without being listed, and listing them would add nothing.

    It would also do harm. Enumerating projects from shared/ pulls their sibling test projects
    into this directory's test run, which contaminates the Cobertura report that the 80 percent
    line coverage gate is measured from, and that gate is evaluated per service precisely so that
    one service's coverage cannot mask another's.

    No peer service project appears here either. That is what keeps the four services decomposed
    rather than collapsed into a single build: one combined solution is a failure mode for this
    refactor, not a cautious default. Nothing appears for any deferred capability area either.
    Those are represented solely as reserved routes on the Gateway, and a routing declaration is
    not a project, so there is no entry, no placeholder and no commented out line for any of them.

    NOTHING INHERITED IS RESTATED HERE
    Target framework, language version, nullable context, implicit usings, warnings as errors and
    central package management all arrive from the repository root Directory.Build.props by
    MSBuild directory walk up. Every package version arrives from the repository root
    Directory.Packages.props. The SDK is pinned by the repository root global.json. This file
    therefore declares project membership and nothing else: no configuration, no property and no
    version. Duplicating any of it here is how four services silently drift apart later.

    PROVENANCE: NO LEGACY BUILD DEFINITION IS TRANSLATED
    The nearest legacy analogue is the PowerBuilder workspace pfw.pbw, which likewise does no more
    than enumerate its child targets by relative path. Nothing here is translated from the two
    PowerBuilder project objects under ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/, because neither is usable:
    project.srj declares 29 libraries under COM:Appeon and includes pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl, while
    p_pfw.srj declares 28 under COM:Sybase, Inc., omits that library, and embeds developer
    workstation absolute paths together with the retired library name pfwUtils.pbl. Both declare
    pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which does not exist anywhere in the repository, so neither would
    build as written and both are read as REFERENCE for build INTENT only. The legacy tree is the
    read only behavioural oracle for parity testing and is never a build input.

    RULES POSITION
    No user rules were provided for this repository: the rules document contains exactly one line
    saying so. Nothing is invented or back filled from convention in their place. The binding
    constraints are the enterprise standard baseline together with the named non rule constraints,
    and each decision recorded above cites the concern that drives it.
  -->

  <!--
    The shippable ASP.NET Core gRPC application. This is the project that carries the four
    ProjectReference edges into shared/, so it is the path by which every shared library enters
    this build without appearing in this file.
  -->
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Persistence/PowerFramework.Persistence.csproj" />

  <!--
    The xunit.v3 test project, and the measured unit for this service's coverage gate: it is the
    project whose run emits the Cobertura report. It references the application project above and
    nothing else, which is why the two entries here are sufficient to build and test the service
    in full.
  -->
  <Project Path="PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests/PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests.csproj" />

</Solution>
